@page "/settings"
@page "/pv-system"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@inject MqttDisplayOptions Mqtt
@inject ApiDisplayOptions Api
@inject NavigationManager Nav

@*
    Settings: the installation (issue #111). The form at the top (issue #204) edits the configuration the
    *next* start reads: a save goes to the web UI's overrides file, a restart applies it, and until then
    each field says what is still running. See PvSystemEditorForm. The Solcast account (issue #286) and
    the tariff (issue #265) below it are the exceptions: the services that use them read them afresh, so
    a save applies at once.

    The MQTT and API sections are read-only, and not merely for now: they are resolved once at startup,
    so a control that edited them would be editing a copy of a decision already made. They answer
    "what is this talking to, and how do I reach it?" from a browser, the question the startup log
    answers once and then buries. Since the redesign (#255) the explanations sit in (i) tips; what
    reports a state -- a link that is off, a key withheld -- stays on the page. The car's facts and
    limits live on the Car page.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="Settings" />

<h1>Settings</h1>

<SettingsTabs Current="SettingsTab.Installation" />

<PvSystemEditorForm />

<SolcastAccountForm />

<TariffEditorForm />

<p class="muted car-link">The car's settings and charging limits are on the <a href="/car">Car</a> page.</p>

<div class="section-head tip-head">
    <h2>MQTT</h2>
    <InfoTip Topic="MQTT" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
        <p>
            Home Assistant is a <em>control</em> surface: the topics ending <code>/set</code> can start and
            stop charging. Vehicle telemetry only listens, and the worst a wrong setting there can do is
            leave the car's card empty.
        </p>
        <p>
            None of this says whether either link is <em>up</em>. Everything on this page was read once at
            startup; <a href="/settings/health">Health</a> reports the polling loop rather than the broker,
            so until it reports this too, the broker's own log — or Home Assistant showing the device as
            unavailable — is what answers that.
        </p>
    </InfoTip>
</div>

@*
    The third and fourth things this controller is talking to (issue #143). The installation form
    above is what it drives; this is what it talks through, and unlike a Modbus address none of it is guessable:
    the prefix everything is published under is composed at startup from two separate settings.

    Two links rather than one broker, kept apart on purpose. They are separately optional -- you can
    publish to Home Assistant without reading the car, or the reverse -- and they may well be two
    different brokers, so a single row saying "the broker" would be wrong on any installation that
    took that up.
*@

<div class="section-head tip-head minor">
    <h3>Home Assistant</h3>
    @if (Mqtt.HomeAssistant.Connection.Enabled)
    {
        <InfoTip Topic="Home Assistant" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
            <p>
                The device id is the root of every entity's <code>unique_id</code>, and it is the one value
                here that is expensive to change: Home Assistant keys an entity to its <code>unique_id</code>,
                so a new one is a new entity, created beside the old one, and every graph starts again. The
                topics and the names can be changed freely. The status interval is how often the state topic
                is republished, which is usually the answer to "why is the Home Assistant dashboard behind?".
            </p>
            <p>
                Every other entity — each number, select, text and button — follows the same pattern:
                <code>@($"{Mqtt.HomeAssistant.TopicPrefix}/{{object_id}}/state")</code> for what it reports and
                <code>@($"{Mqtt.HomeAssistant.TopicPrefix}/{{object_id}}/set")</code> for what it accepts. The
                retained discovery configs Home Assistant reads them from live under
                <code>@($"{Mqtt.HomeAssistant.DiscoveryPrefix}/…/{Mqtt.HomeAssistant.DeviceId}/…/config")</code>.
                Anything marked <em>in</em> can change what the charger is doing.
            </p>
        </InfoTip>
    }
</div>

@if (!Mqtt.HomeAssistant.Connection.Enabled)
{
    <p class="muted" id="ha-mqtt-off">
        Off, which is the default: nothing is published and no command is accepted. Set
        <code>HomeAssistant__Enabled</code> to <code>true</code>, give it
        <code>HomeAssistant__Username</code> and <code>HomeAssistant__Password</code> for the broker,
        and claim a <code>Pv__Id</code> — the id is the topic segment two installations on one broker
        would otherwise overwrite each other on, so the controller refuses to start without it.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <MqttConnection Connection="Mqtt.HomeAssistant.Connection" />

        <dt>Discovery prefix</dt>
        <dd><code>@Mqtt.HomeAssistant.DiscoveryPrefix</code></dd>

        <dt>Device id</dt>
        <dd><code>@Mqtt.HomeAssistant.DeviceId</code></dd>

        <dt>Topic prefix</dt>
        <dd><code>@Mqtt.HomeAssistant.TopicPrefix</code></dd>

        <dt>Status interval</dt>
        <dd>@Interval(Mqtt.HomeAssistant.StatusInterval)</dd>
    </dl>

    <table class="sessions topics">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Carries</th>
                <th>Topic</th>
                <th>Direction</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var topic in Mqtt.HomeAssistant.Topics)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@topic.Purpose</td>
                    <td class="topic"><code>@topic.Topic</code></td>
                    <td class="flow">@(topic.Inbound ? "in" : "out")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    @if (Mqtt.HomeAssistant.IsRetiringAnything)
    {
        <p class="table-note muted" id="ha-mqtt-retiring">
            On every connect this installation also <strong>blanks</strong> the retained messages left
            by a name it used to publish under:
            @if (Mqtt.HomeAssistant.RetiredDeviceIds.Count > 0)
            {
                <text>discovery configs for
                    @string.Join(", ", Mqtt.HomeAssistant.RetiredDeviceIds)</text>
                @(Mqtt.HomeAssistant.RetiredTopicPrefixes.Count > 0 ? "; " : ". ")
            }
            @if (Mqtt.HomeAssistant.RetiredTopicPrefixes.Count > 0)
            {
                <text>retained state under
                    @string.Join(", ", Mqtt.HomeAssistant.RetiredTopicPrefixes). </text>
            }
            Set after a rename, and safe to remove once it has run.
        </p>
    }
}

<div class="section-head tip-head minor">
    <h3>Vehicle telemetry</h3>
    @if (Mqtt.Vehicle.Connection.Enabled)
    {
        <InfoTip Topic="vehicle telemetry" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
            <p>
                One concrete topic, not a wildcard: this phase tracks one car, and a <code>+</code> across
                several would quietly let the newest message win whichever car it described. The messages
                are expected to be <strong>retained</strong>, so a restart is handed the last reading
                straight away instead of waiting for a cloud-fed car's next update. Note that this client id
                is not namespaced by the system's id, so two controllers on one machine would collide on the
                broker.
            </p>
        </InfoTip>
    }
</div>

@if (!Mqtt.Vehicle.Connection.Enabled)
{
    <p class="muted" id="vehicle-mqtt-off">
        Off, which is the default: the car's own state of charge is not read, and the Overview shows
        only what the charger can see. Set <code>Vehicle__Enabled</code> to <code>true</code> and name
        the topic your publisher writes to under
        <code>Ev__Vehicles__0__Telemetry__Topic</code>.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <MqttConnection Connection="Mqtt.Vehicle.Connection" />

        <dt>Subscribed to</dt>
        <dd>
            @if (Mqtt.Vehicle.IsEnabledWithoutTopic)
            {
                <span class="muted">nothing</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <code>@Mqtt.Vehicle.Topic</code>
            }
        </dd>

        <dt>Stale after</dt>
        <dd>@Interval(Mqtt.Vehicle.MaxAge)</dd>

        <dt>Retry every</dt>
        <dd>@Interval(Mqtt.Vehicle.ReconnectInterval)</dd>
    </dl>

    @if (Mqtt.Vehicle.IsEnabledWithoutTopic)
    {
        <p class="table-note muted" id="vehicle-mqtt-no-topic">
            The feed is on with nothing to subscribe to, so it connects to no broker and reads nothing
            — which from the Overview is indistinguishable from a car that never reports. Set
            <code>Ev__Vehicles__0__Telemetry__Topic</code> to the topic your publisher writes to.
        </p>
    }

    @if (Mqtt.SharesOneBroker)
    {
        <p class="table-note muted" id="mqtt-one-broker">
            Both links use the same broker, <code>@Mqtt.Vehicle.Connection.Broker</code>. They are still
            two connections with two client ids, and either can be pointed elsewhere without touching
            the other.
        </p>
    }
}

<div class="section-head tip-head">
    <h2>API</h2>
    @if (Api.Enabled)
    {
        <InfoTip Topic="API" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
            <p>
                The address is built from the one this page arrived on rather than from <code>Web:Port</code>:
                a hostname or a reverse proxy in front means the configured port is not necessarily the one
                that works, while the address that just delivered this page demonstrably does. Both links
                answer without a key by design — a browser cannot send one, and neither carries anything to
                act on — so opening either is the quickest proof that the API is up.
            </p>
        </InfoTip>
    }
</div>

@*
    The third control surface (issue #142), and the one whose whole configuration lives in a file on
    the Pi. Turning it on is a line in .env and a key from `openssl rand -hex 32`; after that the only
    confirmation it worked is a line in the startup log, and the only place the key exists is that
    file. Anyone pointing an MCP server, a script or a dashboard at this installation needs exactly two
    things -- the base URL and a key -- and used to get them by ssh'ing to the Pi and grepping .env.

    Read-only for the same reason as everything above: ApiOptions is bound once at startup, the
    keyless-API check has already refused what could not be honoured, and the routes are mapped -- or
    not mapped -- from it. A control that generated a key here would be editing a copy of a settled
    decision.
*@

@if (!Api.Enabled)
{
    <p class="muted" id="api-off">
        Off, which is the default: no route is mapped and no document is served, so there is nothing to
        find rather than something that answers 401. Two of its endpoints write to hardware, which is
        why it is switched on knowingly — with <code>Api__Enabled=true</code> and at least one key,
        <code>Api__Keys__&lt;name&gt;=$(openssl rand -hex 32)</code>. Enabled with no key is a startup
        failure rather than an open control surface.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Base URL</dt>
        <dd>
            <code>@_api.BaseUrl</code>
            @if (_api.PortIsImplicit)
            {
                <span class="muted">
                    — this page arrived on the default port, so the direct LAN address is
                    <code>host:@Api.Port</code> whatever is in front of it.
                </span>
            }
        </dd>

        <dt>Index</dt>
        <dd><a href="@_api.BaseUrl">@Api.BasePath</a></dd>

        <dt>OpenAPI document</dt>
        <dd><a href="@_api.DocumentUrl">@Api.DocumentPath</a></dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="section-head tip-head minor">
        <h3>Keys</h3>
        <InfoTip Topic="API keys" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
            <p>
                The name is not a credential — it is what reaches the log and the recorded charging session
                as the source of an action, so a charge started through this API is filed against the name
                that started it rather than anonymously. Which is the whole reason several clients get
                several keys.
            </p>
        </InfoTip>
    </div>

    <table class="sessions">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Name</th>
                <th>Key</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var key in Api.Keys)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><code>@key.Name</code></td>
                    <td>
                        @if (key.Secret is { } secret)
                        {
                            <Secret Value="@secret" />
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="muted">configured</span>
                        }
                    </td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    @if (Api.SecretsWithheld)
    {
        <p class="table-note muted" id="api-keys-withheld">
            The keys themselves are not shown, because this UI has no login: anyone who can reach it
            could read them, and a key is bearer-equivalent to the stop button on the wallbox.
            Configure <code>Web__PasswordHash</code> (<code>hash-password</code>) and they become
            readable here.
        </p>
    }

    <div class="section-head tip-head minor">
        <h3>Calling it</h3>
        <InfoTip Topic="calling the API" Group="settings-info" Wide="true">
            <p>
                The first key, this installation's own address and the endpoint worth trying first.
                Everything else is under the same base URL and takes the same header; the document above is
                the list.
            </p>
        </InfoTip>
    </div>

    <p class="code-line">
        @if (Api.Keys[0].Secret is { } example)
        {
            <Secret Value="@example" Before="@CurlPrefix" After="@CurlSuffix" />
        }
        else
        {
            <code>@CurlPrefix&lt;key&gt;@CurlSuffix</code>
        }
    </p>

}

@code {
    // The example call the section exists to serve, split around the key so the secret can be masked
    // inside it while Copy still hands over the whole command.
    private const string CurlPrefix = "curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer ";

    private string CurlSuffix => $"\" {_api.BaseUrl}/status";

    // Where the API is, as seen from the browser that fetched this page -- resolved once, since the
    // address cannot change under a rendered page.
    private ApiAddressDisplay _api = new(string.Empty, string.Empty, PortIsImplicit: true);

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _api = Api.AddressFrom(Nav.BaseUri);

    // Intervals here run from fifteen seconds to twelve hours, and each reads in the units it
    // was configured in: Units.Duration keeps seconds below ten minutes and minutes below two days.
    private static string Interval(TimeSpan value) => value <= TimeSpan.Zero ? Units.Missing : Units.Duration(value);
}
